<Layout title="Psychedelic Neuroplasticity, Relational Frameworks, and Nature Relatedness: An Evidence-Based Integrative Review">
    <header>
      <h1>Psychedelic Neuroplasticity, Relational Frameworks, and Nature Relatedness: An Evidence-Based Integrative Review</h1>
      <div class="metadata">
        <time datetime="2026-09-28">Sep 28, 2026</time>
        <span class="separator" aria-hidden="true">•</span>
        <span class="author">Shasta Fox</span>
      </div>
    </header><blockquote><strong>Integrative review.</strong> This article replaces the earlier essay on relational connection and ecological stewardship. Selected citation details have been corrected; full reference verification and independent peer review remain pending. The Gómez (2026) reference is incomplete and awaits verification.</blockquote>

    <div>
      <section>
        <p class="lead-paragraph">Research on psychedelic neuroplasticity, family systems, relational approaches to psychotherapy, and human relationships with the natural environment can be considered together without treating them as a single causal system. The strongest evidence concerns the pharmacology and plasticity-related effects of classic serotonergic psychedelics, including lysergic acid diethylamide (LSD). Human clinical research also provides preliminary evidence of therapeutic effects for particular psychiatric symptoms under controlled conditions. Separately, Bowen family systems theory and Relational-Cultural Therapy (RCT) provide psychological frameworks for examining differentiation, emotional interdependence, relational connection, and interpersonal context. Research on nature relatedness constitutes another distinct literature concerning human identification with nature and environmental behavior.</p>
        <p>An evidence-based synthesis must preserve the boundaries among these literatures. Evidence that a psychedelic changes neural processes does not by itself demonstrate a change in family functioning. Evidence of changes in family or psychological functioning does not demonstrate ecological behavior. Each proposed connection must therefore be treated according to the level of evidence available.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>LSD and Neuroplasticity: What Is Established</h2>
        <p>Classic serotonergic psychedelics such as LSD act importantly through serotonin 5-HT2A receptor signaling. Preclinical research demonstrates that several psychedelic compounds can alter cellular processes associated with structural and functional neural plasticity. Ly et al. (2018), using cellular and animal models, reported increased neuritogenesis, dendritic spinogenesis, synaptogenesis, and synaptic function following exposure to serotonergic psychedelics. Their experiments also implicated 5-HT2A, TrkB, and mTOR signaling in these plasticity-related effects.</p>
        <p>These results constitute strong mechanistic evidence that psychedelic compounds can influence neural plasticity. Their translational limits are equally important. Much of the foundational evidence comes from cultured neurons and animal models rather than direct measurement of structural synaptic change in living human brains (Ly et al., 2018; Olson, 2022).</p>
        <p>Olson (2022) reviewed the biochemical mechanisms associated with psychedelic-induced neuroplasticity and identified plasticity as a plausible mechanism through which psychedelic effects might persist after the acute pharmacological state has ended. This remains a mechanistic explanation under active investigation rather than proof that all enduring psychological effects of psychedelics are caused by neuroplasticity.</p>
        <p>Human-derived experimental models provide complementary evidence. Costa et al. (2024) exposed human cerebral organoids to LSD and observed changes in proteins associated with proteostasis, energy metabolism, and neuroplasticity-related pathways. Complementary experiments also showed enhanced neurite outgrowth. Cerebral organoids provide a useful human-derived model of cellular processes but are not equivalent to an intact human brain and cannot establish therapeutic or behavioral outcomes.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Human Evidence for Plasticity-Related Change</h2>
        <p>Evidence for psychedelic-associated neuroplasticity in living humans remains more limited than the preclinical literature.</p>
        <p>Murphy et al. (2024) studied 80 healthy male participants randomized to 10 &mu;g LSD or placebo and used an EEG visual long-term potentiation paradigm as an indirect measure of neural plasticity. Conventional event-related-potential analyses did not demonstrate an LSD-related change in visually induced long-term potentiation, either acutely or after repeated microdosing. Dynamic causal modeling of the full waveform, however, identified differences between LSD and placebo in modeled laminar connectivity within visual cortex.</p>
        <p>This distinction is important. Murphy et al. (2024) provide evidence of LSD-associated differences in modeled cortical connectivity during a plasticity-related task, but their results do not constitute direct evidence that LSD increased conventional electrophysiological LTP in humans. The authors themselves emphasized the need for replication and caution in interpreting the modeling results.</p>
        <p>Avram et al. (2026) provide a different type of human evidence. Within a clinical trial of patients with major depressive disorder, diffusion tensor imaging data were available for 35 participants. Participants receiving moderate-to-high-dose LSD showed increases in fractional anisotropy in several white-matter regions compared with the low-dose condition. Within the higher-dose group, post-treatment fractional-anisotropy values were associated with subsequent reductions in depressive symptoms.</p>
        <p>Fractional anisotropy is an MRI-derived measure sensitive to properties of white-matter microstructure. It should not be treated as a direct measurement of dendritic growth, synaptogenesis, or myelination. The Avram et al. (2026) results therefore provide evidence of treatment-associated white-matter microstructural change and an association with clinical improvement, while the underlying cellular mechanisms remain unresolved.</p>
        <p>Calder and Hasler (2023) accordingly describe psychedelic-induced neuroplasticity as a promising but still developing field. Questions remain concerning dose, timing, brain region, duration, translation from animal models to humans, and the relationship between biological plasticity and therapeutic outcome.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Plasticity Does Not Determine the Direction of Change</h2>
        <p>Neuroplasticity is the capacity of a nervous system to change. It is not synonymous with psychological improvement.</p>
        <p>A biological state that permits increased modification of neural processes does not determine which beliefs, behaviors, emotional responses, or relationships will subsequently develop. The environmental and psychological context in which learning occurs therefore remains relevant.</p>
        <p>The REBUS model proposed by Carhart-Harris and Friston (2019) offers one theoretical explanation of psychedelic effects within predictive-processing theory. In this model, psychedelics reduce the precision or influence of certain high-level prior beliefs, allowing information that would ordinarily receive less weight to exert greater influence on cognition and perception.</p>
        <p>REBUS is useful as a theoretical and computational framework. It should not, however, be treated as a proven law of psychedelic action or as proof that particular maladaptive beliefs necessarily disappear during a psychedelic experience.</p>
        <p>The strongest evidence-based formulation is therefore limited: psychedelic exposure can alter neural activity, and substantial preclinical evidence indicates effects on plasticity-related processes. Whether these biological effects produce durable psychological change, what direction that change takes, and how strongly context determines the outcome remain empirical questions.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Clinical Evidence: LSD and Anxiety</h2>
        <p>Human clinical research supports investigation of LSD-assisted therapy for particular psychiatric symptoms, but it does not support broad claims that LSD heals relational or intergenerational dysfunction.</p>
        <p>Holze et al. (2023) conducted a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled phase II trial involving 42 patients experiencing anxiety with or without a life-threatening illness. Participants received LSD or placebo within a structured therapeutic protocol. LSD treatment was associated with significant reductions in anxiety symptoms, with improvements observed during follow-up. The study also reported reductions in measures of comorbid depressive symptoms.</p>
        <p>These results provide controlled clinical evidence that LSD-assisted treatment may reduce anxiety in a selected clinical population. They do not establish that LSD independently produces those outcomes outside the study context, nor do they demonstrate effects on differentiation of self, family triangles, emotional cutoff, relational mutuality, or environmental behavior.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Bowen Family Systems Theory and Differentiation of Self</h2>
        <p>Bowen family systems theory conceptualizes individual functioning within systems of emotional interdependence. Its most extensively researched construct is differentiation of self, which concerns an individual's capacity to maintain thoughtful functioning and emotional regulation while remaining connected to significant relationships, particularly under conditions of anxiety.</p>
        <p>Calatrava et al. (2022) conducted a scoping review of 295 primary studies examining differentiation of self and related Bowen hypotheses. Across the literature, higher differentiation was associated with better psychological health and marital quality, with additional positive associations reported for physical health and intergenerational relationships.</p>
        <p>These findings provide meaningful empirical support for differentiation of self as a psychological construct associated with important measures of functioning. They provide substantially less support for strong causal interpretations.</p>
        <p>Calatrava et al. (2022) found a paucity of longitudinal and causal research examining the stability and intergenerational transmission of differentiation. Evidence concerning the ability of clinical interventions to modify differentiation and thereby improve well-being also remained insufficient.</p>
        <p>Accordingly, Bowen family systems theory can inform hypotheses about relational functioning, but current evidence does not justify describing multigenerational transmission as an established deterministic process.</p>
        <p>More importantly for psychedelic research, none of the psychedelic studies examined here directly measured Bowenian detriangulation, differentiation of self, emotional cutoff, or multigenerational transmission as treatment outcomes. A claim that LSD improves these specific family-system processes would therefore exceed the available evidence.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Relational-Cultural Therapy as a Psychological Framework</h2>
        <p>Relational-Cultural Therapy emphasizes psychological development through connection and examines relationships within broader social and cultural contexts. Frey (2013) describes RCT as an organized theoretical and clinical framework concerned with relational development, therapeutic relationships, cultural context, power, privilege, marginalization, and relational competencies.</p>
        <p>More recently, G&oacute;mez (2026) identifies relational connection as central to RCT and describes authenticity, mutuality, collaboration, and relational engagement as core elements of the framework. G&oacute;mez also identifies continuing opportunities for empirical intervention research, including work involving couples, families, and adolescents.</p>
        <p>RCT can therefore provide a theoretically coherent framework for studying relationships during or following psychedelic-assisted treatment. It cannot currently be described as a demonstrated mechanism through which psychedelics exert their effects.</p>
        <p>No controlled study cited in this review demonstrates that LSD specifically dissolves RCT &ldquo;strategies of disconnection,&rdquo; generates mutuality, or produces the relational outcomes proposed by RCT.</p>
        <p>A more defensible research question is whether relational variables measured before and after psychedelic-assisted treatment change over time and whether those changes are associated with treatment outcomes. That proposition is testable. Presuming the change before measurement is not.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>An Evidence-Based Integrative Model</h2>
        <p>The current evidence supports several separate propositions.</p>
        <p>First, serotonergic psychedelics alter acute brain function through pharmacological mechanisms involving 5-HT2A receptor signaling.</p>
        <p>Second, cellular and animal research provides substantial evidence that psychedelic compounds can influence structural and functional plasticity-related processes (Ly et al., 2018; Olson, 2022).</p>
        <p>Third, emerging human studies have identified neurophysiological and neuroimaging changes compatible with altered plasticity, although these findings are methodologically indirect and remain limited in number (Avram et al., 2026; Murphy et al., 2024).</p>
        <p>Fourth, controlled clinical research demonstrates that LSD-assisted treatment can alter particular psychiatric symptom measures in selected clinical populations (Holze et al., 2023).</p>
        <p>What has not been established is a direct sequence from psychedelic-induced neural plasticity to a particular relational outcome.</p>
        <p>Bowen family systems theory and RCT therefore belong at the level of hypothesis generation and clinical interpretation rather than established psychedelic mechanism. Future research could directly examine whether psychedelic-assisted interventions are followed by measurable changes in differentiation of self, family functioning, relational avoidance, mutuality, or other validated relational outcomes.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Psychedelics and Nature Relatedness</h2>
        <p>The relationship between psychedelic experience and the natural environment has a genuine empirical literature, but the evidence is substantially narrower than a claim that psychedelics produce ecological stewardship.</p>
        <p>Forstmann and Sagioglou (2017) examined 1,487 participants in a cross-sectional online study. Lifetime experience with classic psychedelics was associated with greater self-reported pro-environmental behavior, and statistical modeling indicated that nature relatedness accounted for part of this relationship. The study controlled for several personality characteristics and experience with other classes of psychoactive substances.</p>
        <p>Because participants were not randomly assigned to psychedelic exposure, the study cannot establish that psychedelics caused either greater nature relatedness or pro-environmental behavior. Preexisting differences between people who choose to use psychedelics and those who do not remain a possible explanation.</p>
        <p>Kettner et al. (2019) used a prospective observational design and reported increases in nature relatedness following psychedelic experiences. Changes in nature relatedness were associated with characteristics of the experience and its context, including the degree of ego dissolution and contact with natural surroundings.</p>
        <p>The prospective design provides stronger temporal evidence than a purely cross-sectional association, but it still does not establish causality. The authors explicitly recognized this limitation.</p>
        <p>The strongest conclusion from these two studies is therefore that psychedelic experience is associated with nature relatedness, and prospective observational evidence suggests that nature relatedness can change following psychedelic experiences. A causal effect has not been established.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Nature Connection and Environmental Behavior</h2>
        <p>The second part of the ecological hypothesis has a broader evidence base independent of psychedelic research.</p>
        <p>Mackay and Schmitt (2019) conducted a meta-analysis examining nature connection and pro-environmental behavior. They found a robust correlation between feeling connected with nature and engaging in pro-environmental behavior. Experimental studies produced smaller effects than correlational studies, although a small experimental effect remained when unpublished studies were included.</p>
        <p>This distinction matters.</p>
        <p>The literature provides substantial evidence that nature connection and pro-environmental behavior are related. It provides more limited evidence that experimentally increasing nature connection produces corresponding behavioral change.</p>
        
        <div class="logic-callout">
          <p>Consequently, the available evidence does not justify the causal statement:</p>
          <p><strong>psychedelic exposure &rarr; nature connection &rarr; ecological stewardship</strong></p>
          <p style="margin-top: 0.75rem;">It supports a more limited set of observations:</p>
          <p><em>Psychedelic experience is associated with nature relatedness</em>, and <em>nature relatedness is associated with pro-environmental behavior</em>.</p>
        </div>

        <p>Whether the first relationship causes the second remains an appropriate subject for future research.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>What Current Evidence Does Not Establish</h2>
        <p>Current research does not establish that LSD heals intergenerational family dysfunction.</p>
        <p>It does not establish that psychedelic neuroplasticity increases differentiation of self, dissolves family triangles, reverses emotional cutoff, or resolves RCT-defined relational disconnection.</p>
        <p>It does not establish that LSD creates ecological responsibility or produces durable environmental behavior.</p>
        <p>It also does not establish a single biological mechanism connecting psychedelic-induced plasticity, relational functioning, and environmental attitudes.</p>
        <p>These possibilities can be formulated as hypotheses, but each would require direct empirical testing.</p>
        <p>An adequate research design would need to distinguish multiple levels of measurement: psychedelic exposure and dose, acute subjective effects, neural or physiological measures, psychological flexibility, validated family or relational measures, nature relatedness, and objectively defined environmental behavior. Longitudinal data would be necessary to establish temporal order, and controlled designs would be required for stronger causal inference.</p>
        <p>Evidence of change at one level cannot by itself establish change at another.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Safety and Methodological Restraint</h2>
        <p>Evidence concerning safety also requires attention to research context.</p>
        <p>Hinkle et al. (2024) synthesized studies of classic psychedelics in clinical and laboratory environments and examined the incidence of adverse events. Serious adverse events were rarely reported among healthy volunteers but occurred in approximately 4% of participants with preexisting neuropsychiatric disorders, with nonserious adverse effects (such as transient blood pressure elevations, headaches, nausea, and acute anxiety) occurring regularly.</p>
        <p>The authors concluded that psychedelics were generally well tolerated with a relatively low incidence of serious adverse events under controlled conditions with appropriate participant-selection criteria.</p>
        <p>That qualification is essential. Safety findings from screened research participants receiving monitored administration cannot automatically be generalized to unsupervised use, different doses, people excluded from trials, or populations with substantially different medical or psychiatric characteristics.</p>
        <p>Clinical studies also commonly combine drug administration with preparation, monitoring, therapeutic support, or post-session integration. Unless a study experimentally separates these components, it cannot determine the independent contribution of each one.</p>
        <p>Consequently, therapeutic frameworks such as RCT should be empirically tested within psychedelic treatment rather than assumed to be effective because their theories appear compatible with neuroplasticity.</p>
      </section>

      <section>
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>The strongest evidence-based integration of these literatures begins by keeping their levels of explanation distinct.</p>
        <p>Serotonergic psychedelics such as LSD alter brain function through pharmacological mechanisms involving 5-HT2A signaling. Cellular and animal studies provide substantial evidence of effects on neural plasticity, while emerging human research identifies neurophysiological and white-matter changes that are compatible with&mdash;but do not by themselves prove&mdash;the same cellular mechanisms in humans (Avram et al., 2026; Murphy et al., 2024). Separately, Bowen family systems theory and Relational-Cultural Therapy offer rich clinical frameworks for conceptualizing differentiation, emotional reactivity, and growth-fostering connection, yet their relevance to psychedelic outcomes remains at the level of hypotheses awaiting direct empirical evaluation. Similarly, while psychedelic experience is associated with nature relatedness, and nature relatedness correlates with pro-environmental behavior, current data do not substantiate an automatic causal pipeline from receptor agonism to ecological stewardship. By maintaining rigorous boundaries among cellular pharmacology, relational models, and environmental psychology, researchers can construct testable, multi-level designs that respect both the empirical strengths and the epistemic limits of each field.</p>
      </section>

